arXiv:2610.27172v1 Announce Type: cross 
Abstract: In this work, we study numerical approximations of the equation \begin{equation}
  \partial_t^\alpha u-\nabla\cdot\left(u^{m-1}\nabla(-\Delta)^{-\sigma}u\right)=0, \end{equation} where $0<\alpha<1$, $0<\sigma<1$ and $m>1$. The Caputo derivative is discretized by the L1 formula, while the nonlinear nonlocal flux is approximated by a conservative upwind finite-difference scheme. We establish several structural properties of the implicit scheme in arbitrary space dimension, including a discrete maximum principle, non-negativity, mass conservation, $\ell^\infty$ and $\ell^2$ stability, existence of non-negative discrete solutions, and a discrete energy-dissipation estimate. Our assumptions include natural symmetry, a sign condition on the sum of the directional coefficients, and decay on the discrete weights. We also derive a consistency estimate for the complete nonlinear spatial operator. For rigorous convergence analysis, we restrict ourselves to one space dimension and introduce the cumulative variable associated with the density. This transforms the discrete problem into a monotone approximation of an equation involving the fractional Laplacian. Interestingly, for semigroup-based pressure-gradient weights, the resulting discrete operator is identified with the fractional discrete Laplacian. Next, using comparison and contraction properties of the integrated scheme, for $m\geq2$ and under the stated spatial regularity and weighted temporal consistency assumptions, we prove uniform convergence of the cumulative numerical solution and convergence of the corresponding density against compactly supported smooth test functions. The temporal estimates allow a weak singularity at the initial time. We also analyze an explicit variant of the scheme and derive a sufficient CFL condition covering the full range $0<\sigma<1$.

arXiv:2505.19021v3 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: We investigate the qualitative properties of positive classical solutions $u$ to critical Hartree equations on punctured domains, assuming that $u(x)\to+\infty$ as $x\to0$. For the global problem set in $\mathbb R^n\setminus\{0\}$, we prove that such solutions are radial and nonincreasing in $|x|$. For the local problem in $B_R\setminus\{0\}$, we establish the upper bound $u(x)\leqslant C|x|^{-(n-2)/2}$ near the origin and the asymptotic radial symmetry estimate
  \begin{equation*}
  u(x)=\bar u(|x|)\bigl(1+\mathcal O(|x|)\bigr)\qquad\text{as }x\to0,
  \end{equation*}
  where $\bar u$ is the spherical average of $u$. Our approach combines integral representations, blow-up analysis, and the method of moving spheres. A main difficulty is controlling the nonlocal Hartree potential under Kelvin inversion and in the blow-up limit. We also give an alternative proof of the classification of positive entire classical solutions in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is used to identify the blow-up profile.

arXiv:2510.15507v5 Announce Type: replace 
Abstract: We consider the following quasilinear critical problem involving the mixed local-nonlocal operator: \begin{equation}\label{main_prob_abstract_1}\tag{$\mathcal{P}_p$}
  -\Delta_p u+(-\Delta_p)^s u=|u|^{p^*-2}u+f \text{ in }\mathbb{R}^N, \end{equation} where $s \in (0,1), p \in (1, \infty), N>p$, $p^*=\frac{Np}{N-p}$, and $f$ is a nonnegative functional in the dual space of the ambient solution space. If $f \equiv0$, then we show that \eqref{main_prob_abstract_1} does not admit any nontrivial weak solution. This phenomenon stands in contrast to the purely local and purely nonlocal cases. On the other hand, if $f$ is a nontrivial nonnegative functional, we establish the existence of a positive weak solution to \eqref{main_prob_abstract_1} provided $\|f\|$ is small. For this purpose we prove the concentration compactness principle for the mixed operator $-\Delta_p +(-\Delta_p)^s$ in $\mathbb{R}^N$. We also discuss the multiplicity of positive weak solutions to \eqref{main_prob_abstract_1}.

arXiv:2609.09894v2 Announce Type: replace Abstract: We study the Neumann boundary value problem $$ -\operatorname{div}\left( rac{
abla u}{\sqrt{1-|
abla u|^2}}ight) = \lambda a(x)g(u) \quad 	ext{in } \Omega, \qquad  rac{
abla u}{\sqrt{1-|
abla u|^2}}\cdot \mathbf n = 0 \quad 	ext{on } \partial\Omega, $$ where $\Omega \subset \mathbb R^N$ is a bounded convex domain, $a\in L^\infty(\Omega)$ is an indefinite weight with $\int_\Omega a(x) \, dx< 0$, and $g$ is a nonlinearity. Under suitable assumptions on $g$, we prove the existence of two positive solutions for sufficiently large $\lambda>0$, using Szulkin's theory for nonsmooth functionals: a global minimizer $u_\lambda^{(l)}$ with negative energy, and a mountain-pass critical point $u_\lambda^{(s)}$ with positive energy. Furthermore, we study the asymptotic behaviour as $\lambda	o+\infty$ of both families of solutions. For the ground states we prove that, up to a subsequence, $u_\lambda^{(l)}	o u_\infty$ uniformly, where $u_\infty$ satisfies $\|
abla u_\infty\|_{L^\infty(\Omega)}=1$ and is characterized by being a maximizer of $\int_\Omega a(x) G(u) \, dx$ and a minimizer of the kinetic energy among all such maximizers. In the model case $g(u)=|u|^{p-2}u$, $p>2$, we also show that the mountain-pass energy level satisfies the upper bound $c_\lambda=O(\lambda^{-2/(p-2)})$, and that $u_\lambda^{(s)}	o0$ in $C(\overline\Omega)$.
